n:
David’s song of thanksgiving combines three psalms: 

- {{1 Chronicles 16:8-22|First Chronicles 16:8–22}} uses {{Psalm 105:1-15|Psalm 105:1–15}}

- {{1 Chronicles 16:23-33|First Chronicles 16:23–33}} uses {{Psalm 96:1-13|Psalm 96}}

- {{1 Chronicles 16:34-38|First Chronicles 16:34–38}} uses {{Psalm 106:1|Psalm 106:1}}, {{Psalm 106:47-48|47–48}}

The composer adjusted the sources for this occasion. The Temple did not exist when the ark was brought to Jerusalem, so the Chronicler says strength and joy fill his dwelling ({{1 Chronicles 16:27|1 Chronicles 16:27}}) instead of *His sanctuary* ({{Psalm 96:6|Psalm 96:6}}) and asks the worshiper to come into his presence ({{1 Chronicles 16:29|1 Chronicles 16:29}}) instead of *enter His courts* ({{Psalm 96:8|Psalm 96:8}}). The name *Abraham* ({{Psalm 105:9|Psalm 105:9}}) changes to *Israel* ({{1 Chronicles 16:13|1 Chronicles 16:13}}) to focus on the nation as the fulfillment of Abraham's promises. The hymn does not mention the judgment of the earth ({{Psalm 96:10|Psalm 96:10}}, {{Psalm 96:13|13}}; compare {{1 Chronicles 16:30|1 Chronicles 16:30}}, {{1 Chronicles 16:33|33}}) because it was not relevant to the ark's dedication.
